WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.975
We all use data and databases
in our daily online lives.

2
00:00:03.975 --> 00:00:07.830
For example, uploading photos
to our social media feeds,

3
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.570
downloading files at work,

4
00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.010
and playing games online,

5
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.320
are all examples
of database usage.

6
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.270
What exactly is data

7
00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.685
and how does it interact
with the database?

8
00:00:17.685 --> 00:00:19.740
If you're struggling to
answer these questions,

9
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.050
don't worry, by the
end of this video,

10
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.580
you'll be able to describe what

11
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.010
a database is at a
conceptual level,

12
00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.655
identify real-world examples
of the use of databases,

13
00:00:29.655 --> 00:00:31.545
and demonstrate an
understanding of how

14
00:00:31.545 --> 00:00:34.380
data is organized in a database.

15
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.445
Let's return to the first of
our questions. What is data?

16
00:00:38.445 --> 00:00:43.660
In basic terms, data is facts
and figures about anything.

17
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:47.015
For example, if data were
collected on a person,

18
00:00:47.015 --> 00:00:49.115
then that data might
include their name,

19
00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.880
age, email, and date of birth.

20
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.630
Or the data could be facts and

21
00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:55.805
figures related to
an online purchase.

22
00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:58.310
This could be the order
number, description,

23
00:00:58.310 --> 00:00:59.840
order quantity, and date,

24
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.720
and even the customer's email.

25
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.465
Data is crucial for individuals
as well as organizations.

26
00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:07.310
Where's all this data stored?

27
00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.485
In our digital world,

28
00:01:09.485 --> 00:01:12.370
data is no longer
stored in manual files.

29
00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:15.950
Instead, developers use
something called databases.

30
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.420
A database is a form of

31
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:18.830
electronic storage in which

32
00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:20.930
data is organized
systematically.

33
00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.795
It stores and manipulates
data electronically

34
00:01:23.795 --> 00:01:27.310
to make it more manageable,
efficient, and secure.

35
00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.330
There are many
real-world examples

36
00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.045
of where databases are used.

37
00:01:31.045 --> 00:01:33.020
For example, a bank can use

38
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.330
a database to store
data for its customers,

39
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:37.160
bank accounts, and transactions.

40
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.610
A hospital uses a database
to store patient data,

41
00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.895
staff data, laboratory
data, and much more.

42
00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:46.280
At this point, you might
be asking yourself,

43
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.785
but what does a database
actually look like?

44
00:01:48.785 --> 00:01:50.660
Well, a database looks

45
00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.805
like data organized
systematically.

46
00:01:52.805 --> 00:01:54.740
This organization
typically looks

47
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.750
like a spreadsheet or a table.

48
00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.290
What exactly does the
term systematic mean?

49
00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.510
All data contains elements or

50
00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:05.455
features and attributes by
which they can be identified.

51
00:02:05.455 --> 00:02:07.610
For example, a person can be

52
00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.590
identified by attributes
like their age,

53
00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.225
height, or hair color.

54
00:02:11.225 --> 00:02:13.670
This data is separated
and stored in what's

55
00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.625
known as entities that
represent those elements.

56
00:02:16.625 --> 00:02:20.120
As you just learned, an
entity is like a table.

57
00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.250
It contains rows
and columns that

58
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:24.905
store data relating to
a specific element.

59
00:02:24.905 --> 00:02:28.550
In other words, these
are relational elements.

60
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.330
They're related to one another.

61
00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:31.970
These entities could be physical

62
00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:33.845
representations
like an employee,

63
00:02:33.845 --> 00:02:35.390
a customer, or a product.

64
00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:38.060
Or they could be
conceptual like an order,

65
00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.235
an invoice or a quotation.

66
00:02:40.235 --> 00:02:43.415
Entities then store data
in a table-like format

67
00:02:43.415 --> 00:02:44.900
against the attributes or

68
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.715
features related to the element.

69
00:02:46.715 --> 00:02:48.980
For example, an online store

70
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.390
could hold customer's data in

71
00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.220
a customer entity containing

72
00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.740
specific attributes
relating to the customer.

73
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.515
These attributes could
include first name,

74
00:02:57.515 --> 00:03:01.010
last name, date of
birth, and email.

75
00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.440
They could also have
product data stored in

76
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.100
a product entity against
attributes like product code,

77
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.219
description, price,
and availability.

78
00:03:10.219 --> 00:03:12.335
In the relational
database world,

79
00:03:12.335 --> 00:03:15.500
these entities are known
as relations or tables.

80
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.185
The attributes become the
columns of the table.

81
00:03:18.185 --> 00:03:21.550
Each table row represents
an instance of that entity.

82
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.600
As an example, let's take

83
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:24.890
the entities from the online

84
00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:26.825
store example that
you just explored.

85
00:03:26.825 --> 00:03:29.420
These two examples could
be combined into a list of

86
00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.080
orders the store received
from his customers.

87
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.430
Within a database,
this data could be

88
00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:37.070
rendered as an order
table or entity.

89
00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.350
The data can be
organized into rows that

90
00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.815
contain a unique order number.

91
00:03:41.815 --> 00:03:44.420
The name of the customer
who placed the order,

92
00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.010
the product that they ordered,

93
00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:47.845
and the price of that product.

94
00:03:47.845 --> 00:03:51.079
There are many ways to
organize data in a database.

95
00:03:51.079 --> 00:03:53.270
Relational databases
aren't the only kind

96
00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:55.040
of databases that
you'll encounter.

97
00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.735
As a Database Engineer,

98
00:03:56.735 --> 00:03:59.525
you'll work with many
different types of databases.

99
00:03:59.525 --> 00:04:02.935
Here's a few common examples
of other types of databases.

100
00:04:02.935 --> 00:04:06.200
An object-oriented database
is where data is stored

101
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.470
in the form of objects instead
of tables or relations.

102
00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:10.880
An example of this kind of

103
00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.160
database could be an
online bookstore.

104
00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:16.490
The store's database could
render authors, customers,

105
00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:20.305
books, and publishers as classes
like sets or categories.

106
00:04:20.305 --> 00:04:22.310
The objects or instances of

107
00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:25.300
these classes would then
hold the actual data.

108
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:28.985
Graph databases store data
in the form of nodes.

109
00:04:28.985 --> 00:04:31.790
In this case, entities
like customers,

110
00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.775
orders, and products are
represented as nodes.

111
00:04:34.775 --> 00:04:38.605
The relationships between them
are represented as edges.

112
00:04:38.605 --> 00:04:42.560
Finally, there's document
databases where data is stored

113
00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.340
as JSON or JavaScript
Object Notation objects.

114
00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:49.460
The data is organized into
collections like tables.

115
00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.710
Within each collection
a documents

116
00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.085
written in JSON
that record data.

117
00:04:54.085 --> 00:04:57.020
In this example, customer
documents are held in

118
00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.510
a customer collection
while ordering

119
00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:00.920
product documents are stored in

120
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.100
the ordering product
collections.

121
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.140
Where are the databases
themselves stored?

122
00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:07.910
A database can be hosted on

123
00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:09.380
a dedicated machine within

124
00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.150
the premises of an organization,

125
00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:13.130
or it could be
hosted on the Cloud.

126
00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:16.295
Cloud databases are currently
a more popular choice.

127
00:05:16.295 --> 00:05:19.310
This is because they allow
you to store, manage,

128
00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:20.450
and retrieve data to

129
00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.855
a Cloud platform and access
data through the Internet.

130
00:05:23.855 --> 00:05:26.090
They all provide a
lower-cost option for

131
00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:28.570
data management and
other similar options.

132
00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.400
You should now understand
the concept of a database.

133
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.310
You should also be able to
identify examples of databases

134
00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:37.370
and demonstrate how data is
organized within a database.

135
00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.050
Great start. You'll be

136
00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.090
storing and managing
data in no time.